bash 5.3 discarded the saved status of a pid in the table of background pids
(bgp_delete() in make_child()) for every new child, not only for an asynchronous
one, which is what goes into that table: the status of a background job or a
process substitution that had ended could be lost (`wait $pid` answering 127)
when a later foreground child got the same pid. Only a new asynchronous child
discards a saved status now.
The change of make_child() in the devel branch of bash (81d8584, 11.11.2025), as
Gentoo carries it on 5.3_p20 (app-shells/bash files/bash-5.3_p9-pid-reuse.patch,
bugs 965423 and 970713:
https://github.com/gentoo/gentoo/blob/master/app-shells/bash/files/bash-5.3_p9-pid-reuse.patch).

--- jobs.c
+++ jobs.c
@@ -2482,9 +2482,11 @@ make_child (char *command, int flags)
 	 been reused. */
       delete_old_job (pid);
 
-      /* Perform the check for pid reuse unconditionally.  Some systems reuse
-	 PIDs before giving a process CHILD_MAX/_SC_CHILD_MAX unique ones. */
-      bgp_delete (pid);		/* new process, discard any saved status */
+      /* Perform the check for background pid reuse unconditionally.
+	 Some systems reuse PIDs before giving a process
+	 CHILD_MAX/_SC_CHILD_MAX unique ones. */
+      if (async_p)
+	bgp_delete (pid);	/* new background process, discard any saved status */
 
       last_made_pid = pid;
 
